{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-81684" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in $\\text{ft}/{\\text{s}}^{2},$ given by the function $a(t)=6{t}^{2}-4{t}^{3},$ where $t$ is the time in seconds. If the particle has velocity $v=-5\\,\\text{ft}/\\text{s}$ when $t=2\\,\\text{s}$, find the velocity (in $\\text{ft/s}$) of the particle at time $t.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010777/state.json" "file_sha256" "08db95fde8ad568f4c22a52004e0b71ebaee76a9f27a8b88a3cf9c28ea04cc48"} {"question" "q-81684" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (6{t}^{2}-4{t}^{3})\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{t}^{3}-{t}^{4}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(2)=-5.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5 & =2(2)^{3}-(2)^{4}+C \\\\ -5 & =16-16+C \\\\ C & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=2{t}^{3}-{t}^{4}-5.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010777/state.json" "file_sha256" "08db95fde8ad568f4c22a52004e0b71ebaee76a9f27a8b88a3cf9c28ea04cc48"} {"question" "q-81684" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010777/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "97bf1afa6ceed68565479fe95947852ea820cfb8ac2cec4ad8694941a33898d7"} {"question" "q-81684" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010777/state.json" "file_sha256" "08db95fde8ad568f4c22a52004e0b71ebaee76a9f27a8b88a3cf9c28ea04cc48"} {"question" "q-81684" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "v(t)=2{t}^{3}-{t}^{4}+3"] ["math" "v(t)=2{t}^{3}-{t}^{4}-5"] ["math" "v(t)=3{t}^{2}-{t}^{3}-9"] ["math" "v(t)=3{t}^{3}-2{t}^{4}+3"] ["math" "v(t)=6{t}^{3}-4{t}^{4}+11"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010777/state.json" "file_sha256" "08db95fde8ad568f4c22a52004e0b71ebaee76a9f27a8b88a3cf9c28ea04cc48"} {"question" "q-81684" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "v(t)=2{t}^{3}-{t}^{4}-5"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010777/state.json" "file_sha256" "08db95fde8ad568f4c22a52004e0b71ebaee76a9f27a8b88a3cf9c28ea04cc48"} {"question" "q-350978" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in $\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2},$ given by the function $a(t)=14{t}^{6}+12{t}^{3},$ where $t$ is the time in seconds. Find the velocity $v(t)$ (in $\\text{m/s}$) of the particle at time $t$ given that the particle has velocity $v=310\\,\\text{m/s}$ when $t=2\\,\\text{s}.$\n\n$v(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010777/state.json" "file_sha256" "08db95fde8ad568f4c22a52004e0b71ebaee76a9f27a8b88a3cf9c28ea04cc48"} {"question" "q-350978" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (14{t}^{6}+12{t}^{3})\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{t}^{7}+3{t}^{4}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(2)=310.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}310 & =2({2}^{7})+3({2}^{4})+C \\\\ 310 & =304+C \\\\ C & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=2{t}^{7}+3{t}^{4}+6.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010777/state.json" "file_sha256" "08db95fde8ad568f4c22a52004e0b71ebaee76a9f27a8b88a3cf9c28ea04cc48"} {"question" "q-350978" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010777/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "97bf1afa6ceed68565479fe95947852ea820cfb8ac2cec4ad8694941a33898d7"} {"question" "q-350978" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010777/state.json" "file_sha256" "08db95fde8ad568f4c22a52004e0b71ebaee76a9f27a8b88a3cf9c28ea04cc48"} {"question" "q-350978" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2t^7+3t^4+6"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010777/state.json" "file_sha256" "08db95fde8ad568f4c22a52004e0b71ebaee76a9f27a8b88a3cf9c28ea04cc48"} {"question" "q-351011" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in ${\\text{ft/s}}^{2},$ given by the function $a(t)=8{t}^{3}-6{t}^{2},$ where $t$ is the time in seconds. Find the speed of the particle at the moment $t=3\\,\\text{s}$ given that the particle has velocity $v=-4\\,\\text{ft/s}$ when $t=0\\,\\text{s}.$\n\nThe speed at the moment $t=3\\,\\text{s}$ is\n{{field-1}} $\\text{ft/s}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010777/state.json" "file_sha256" "08db95fde8ad568f4c22a52004e0b71ebaee76a9f27a8b88a3cf9c28ea04cc48"} {"question" "q-351011" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (8{t}^{3}-6{t}^{2})\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{t}^{4}-2{t}^{3}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(0)=-4.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4 & =2({0}^{4})-2({0}^{3})+C \\\\ -4 & =0+C \\\\ C & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=2{t}^{4}-2{t}^{3}-4.$\n\nWe evaluate the velocity at time $t=3\\,\\text{s}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(3) & =2({3}^{4})-2({3}^{3})-4 \\\\  & =2(81)-2(27)-4 \\\\  & =162-54-4 \\\\  & =104\\,\\text{ft/s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the speed is equal to $|v(t)|,$ we conclude that the speed of the particle is $104\\,\\text{ft/s}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010777/state.json" "file_sha256" "08db95fde8ad568f4c22a52004e0b71ebaee76a9f27a8b88a3cf9c28ea04cc48"} {"question" "q-351011" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010777/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "97bf1afa6ceed68565479fe95947852ea820cfb8ac2cec4ad8694941a33898d7"} {"question" "q-351011" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010777/state.json" "file_sha256" "08db95fde8ad568f4c22a52004e0b71ebaee76a9f27a8b88a3cf9c28ea04cc48"} {"question" "q-351011" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "104"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010777/state.json" "file_sha256" "08db95fde8ad568f4c22a52004e0b71ebaee76a9f27a8b88a3cf9c28ea04cc48"} {"question" "q-102693" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in ${\\text{m/s}}^{2},$ given by the function $a(t)=3+8t-3{t}^{2}$, where $t$ is the time in seconds. If the particle has velocity $v=-1\\,\\text{m/s}$ when $t=0\\,\\text{s}$, calculate the speed of the particle at the moment $t=2\\,\\text{s}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010777/state.json" "file_sha256" "08db95fde8ad568f4c22a52004e0b71ebaee76a9f27a8b88a3cf9c28ea04cc48"} {"question" "q-102693" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (3+8t-3{t}^{2})\\text{d}t \\\\  & =3t+4{t}^{2}-{t}^{3}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(0)=-1.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & =3(0)+4(0)^{2}-(0)^{3}+C \\\\ -1 & =0+C \\\\ C & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=3t+4{t}^{2}-{t}^{3}-1.$\n\nWe evaluate the velocity at time $t=2\\,\\text{s}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(2) & =3(2)+4(2)^{2}-(2)^{3}-1 \\\\  & =6+16-8-1 \\\\  & =13\\,\\text{m/s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the speed is equal to $|v(t)|,$ we conclude that the speed of the particle is $13\\,\\text{m/s}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010777/state.json" "file_sha256" "08db95fde8ad568f4c22a52004e0b71ebaee76a9f27a8b88a3cf9c28ea04cc48"} {"question" "q-102693" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010777/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "97bf1afa6ceed68565479fe95947852ea820cfb8ac2cec4ad8694941a33898d7"} {"question" "q-102693" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010777/state.json" "file_sha256" "08db95fde8ad568f4c22a52004e0b71ebaee76a9f27a8b88a3cf9c28ea04cc48"} {"question" "q-102693" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "11\\,\\text{m/s}"] ["math" "13\\,\\text{m/s}"] ["math" "15\\,\\text{m/s}"] ["math" "17\\,\\text{m/s}"] ["math" "9\\,\\text{m/s}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010777/state.json" "file_sha256" "08db95fde8ad568f4c22a52004e0b71ebaee76a9f27a8b88a3cf9c28ea04cc48"} {"question" "q-102693" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "13\\,\\text{m/s}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010777/state.json" "file_sha256" "08db95fde8ad568f4c22a52004e0b71ebaee76a9f27a8b88a3cf9c28ea04cc48"} {"question" "q-81474" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in $\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2},$ given by the function $a(t)=6{t}^{2}-2t-10$, where $t$ is the time in seconds. If the particle has velocity $v=-4\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}$ when $t=1\\,\\text{s}$, calculate the speed of the particle at the moment $t=3\\,\\text{s}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010777/state.json" "file_sha256" "08db95fde8ad568f4c22a52004e0b71ebaee76a9f27a8b88a3cf9c28ea04cc48"} {"question" "q-81474" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (6{t}^{2}-2t-10)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{t}^{3}-{t}^{2}-10t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(1)=-4.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4 & =2(1)^{3}-(1)^{2}-10(1)+C \\\\ -4 & =2-1-10+C \\\\ C & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=2{t}^{3}-{t}^{2}-10t+5.$\n\nWe evaluate the velocity at time $t=3\\,\\text{s}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(3) & =2(3)^{3}-(3)^{2}-10(3)+5 \\\\  & =54-9-30+5 \\\\  & =20\\,\\text{m/s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the speed is equal to $|v(t)|,$ we conclude that the speed of the particle is $20\\,\\text{m/s}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010777/state.json" "file_sha256" "08db95fde8ad568f4c22a52004e0b71ebaee76a9f27a8b88a3cf9c28ea04cc48"} {"question" "q-81474" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010777/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "97bf1afa6ceed68565479fe95947852ea820cfb8ac2cec4ad8694941a33898d7"} {"question" "q-81474" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010777/state.json" "file_sha256" "08db95fde8ad568f4c22a52004e0b71ebaee76a9f27a8b88a3cf9c28ea04cc48"} {"question" "q-81474" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "15\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}"] ["math" "16\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}"] ["math" "20\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}"] ["math" "22\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}"] ["math" "24\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010777/state.json" "file_sha256" "08db95fde8ad568f4c22a52004e0b71ebaee76a9f27a8b88a3cf9c28ea04cc48"} {"question" "q-81474" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "20\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010777/state.json" "file_sha256" "08db95fde8ad568f4c22a52004e0b71ebaee76a9f27a8b88a3cf9c28ea04cc48"} {"question" "q-351060" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration $a(t)=-\\frac{4}{(t+1)^{2}},$ where $a(t)$ is measured in ${\\text{m/s}}^{2}$ and $t$ is measured in seconds. Find the speed of the particle at the moment $t=1\\,\\text{s}$ given that the particle has velocity $v=\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{m/s}$ when $t=0\\,\\text{s}.$\n\nThe speed at the moment $t=1\\,\\text{s}$ is\n{{field-1}} $\\,\\text{m/s}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010777/state.json" "file_sha256" "08db95fde8ad568f4c22a52004e0b71ebaee76a9f27a8b88a3cf9c28ea04cc48"} {"question" "q-351060" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int -\\frac{4}{(t+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{t+1}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(0)=\\frac{1}{2}.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} & =\\frac{4}{0+1}+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & =4+C \\\\ C & =-\\frac{7}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=\\frac{4}{t+1}-\\frac{7}{2}.$\n\nWe evaluate the velocity at time $t=1\\,\\text{s}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(1) & =\\frac{4}{1+1}-\\frac{7}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{2}-\\frac{7}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{2}-\\frac{7}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\,\\text{m/s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the speed is equal to $|v(t)|,$ we conclude that the speed of the particle is $\\frac{3}{2}\\,\\text{m/s}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010777/state.json" "file_sha256" "08db95fde8ad568f4c22a52004e0b71ebaee76a9f27a8b88a3cf9c28ea04cc48"} {"question" "q-351060" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010777/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "97bf1afa6ceed68565479fe95947852ea820cfb8ac2cec4ad8694941a33898d7"} {"question" "q-351060" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010777/state.json" "file_sha256" "08db95fde8ad568f4c22a52004e0b71ebaee76a9f27a8b88a3cf9c28ea04cc48"} {"question" "q-351060" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{3}{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010777/state.json" "file_sha256" "08db95fde8ad568f4c22a52004e0b71ebaee76a9f27a8b88a3cf9c28ea04cc48"} {"question" "q-102742" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration $a(t)=\\sqrt{{t}^{3}},$ where $a(t)$ is measured in ${\\text{m/s}}^{2}$ and $t$ is measured in seconds. If the particle has velocity $v=0\\,\\text{m/s}$ when $t=0\\,\\text{s}$, calculate the speed of the particle at the moment $t=1\\,\\text{s}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010777/state.json" "file_sha256" "08db95fde8ad568f4c22a52004e0b71ebaee76a9f27a8b88a3cf9c28ea04cc48"} {"question" "q-102742" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int \\sqrt{{t}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int {t}^{3/2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}{t}^{5/2}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(0)=0.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =\\frac{2}{5}(0)^{5/2}+C \\\\ 0 & =0+C \\\\ C & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=\\frac{2}{5}{t}^{5/2}.$\n\nWe evaluate the velocity at time $t=1\\,\\text{s}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(1) & =\\frac{2}{5}(1)^{5/2}=\\frac{2}{5}\\,\\text{m/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the speed is equal to $|v(t)|,$ we conclude that the speed of the particle is $\\frac{2}{5}\\,\\text{m/s}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010777/state.json" "file_sha256" "08db95fde8ad568f4c22a52004e0b71ebaee76a9f27a8b88a3cf9c28ea04cc48"} {"question" "q-102742" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010777/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "97bf1afa6ceed68565479fe95947852ea820cfb8ac2cec4ad8694941a33898d7"} {"question" "q-102742" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010777/state.json" "file_sha256" "08db95fde8ad568f4c22a52004e0b71ebaee76a9f27a8b88a3cf9c28ea04cc48"} {"question" "q-102742" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{1}{5}\\,\\text{m/s}"] ["math" "\\frac{2}{3}\\,\\text{m/s}"] ["math" "\\frac{2}{5}\\,\\text{m/s}"] ["math" "\\frac{3}{2}\\,\\text{m/s}"] ["math" "\\frac{5}{2}\\,\\text{m/s}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010777/state.json" "file_sha256" "08db95fde8ad568f4c22a52004e0b71ebaee76a9f27a8b88a3cf9c28ea04cc48"} {"question" "q-102742" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{2}{5}\\,\\text{m/s}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010777/state.json" "file_sha256" "08db95fde8ad568f4c22a52004e0b71ebaee76a9f27a8b88a3cf9c28ea04cc48"} {"question" "q-19487" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in $\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2},$ given by the function $a(t)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)$, where $t$ is the time in seconds. If the particle has velocity $v=0\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}$ when $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,\\text{s}$, find the velocity of the particle at time $t=\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\,\\text{s}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010777/state.json" "file_sha256" "08db95fde8ad568f4c22a52004e0b71ebaee76a9f27a8b88a3cf9c28ea04cc48"} {"question" "q-19487" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t))\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =3(\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t))+C \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=0.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})+C \\\\ 0 & =\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi )+C \\\\ C & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the velocity at time $t=\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\,\\text{s}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(\\frac{\\pi }{8}) & =\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{8}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4} \\\\  & =0.75\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010777/state.json" "file_sha256" "08db95fde8ad568f4c22a52004e0b71ebaee76a9f27a8b88a3cf9c28ea04cc48"} {"question" "q-19487" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010777/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "97bf1afa6ceed68565479fe95947852ea820cfb8ac2cec4ad8694941a33898d7"} {"question" "q-19487" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010777/state.json" "file_sha256" "08db95fde8ad568f4c22a52004e0b71ebaee76a9f27a8b88a3cf9c28ea04cc48"} {"question" "q-19487" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0.75\\,\\text{m/s}"] ["math" "0.75\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}"] ["math" "0\\,\\text{m/s}"] ["math" "1.25\\,\\text{m/s}"] ["math" "1.33\\,\\text{m/s}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010777/state.json" "file_sha256" "08db95fde8ad568f4c22a52004e0b71ebaee76a9f27a8b88a3cf9c28ea04cc48"} {"question" "q-19487" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0.75\\,\\text{m/s}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010777/state.json" "file_sha256" "08db95fde8ad568f4c22a52004e0b71ebaee76a9f27a8b88a3cf9c28ea04cc48"} {"question" "q-81710" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in $\\text{ft}/{\\text{s}}^{2},$ given by the function $a(t)=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)$, where $t$ is the time in seconds. If the particle has velocity $v=3\\,\\text{ft}/\\text{s}$ when $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\,\\text{s}$, calculate the speed of the particle at the moment $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,\\text{s}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010777/state.json" "file_sha256" "08db95fde8ad568f4c22a52004e0b71ebaee76a9f27a8b88a3cf9c28ea04cc48"} {"question" "q-81710" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int 4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4(-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))+C \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=3.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3 & =-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4})+C \\\\ 3 & =-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+C \\\\ 3 & =-2\\cdot 0+C \\\\ C & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=3-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t).$\n\nWe evaluate the velocity at time $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,\\text{s}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =3-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =3-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi ) \\\\  & =3-2\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =5\\,\\text{ft}/\\text{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the speed is equal to $|v(t)|,$ we conclude that the speed of the particle is $5\\,\\text{ft}/\\text{s}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010777/state.json" "file_sha256" "08db95fde8ad568f4c22a52004e0b71ebaee76a9f27a8b88a3cf9c28ea04cc48"} {"question" "q-81710" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010777/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "97bf1afa6ceed68565479fe95947852ea820cfb8ac2cec4ad8694941a33898d7"} {"question" "q-81710" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010777/state.json" "file_sha256" "08db95fde8ad568f4c22a52004e0b71ebaee76a9f27a8b88a3cf9c28ea04cc48"} {"question" "q-81710" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1\\,\\text{ft}/\\text{s}"] ["math" "3\\,\\text{ft}/\\text{s}"] ["math" "4\\,\\text{ft}/\\text{s}"] ["math" "5\\,\\text{ft}/\\text{s}"] ["math" "7\\,\\text{ft}/\\text{s}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010777/state.json" "file_sha256" "08db95fde8ad568f4c22a52004e0b71ebaee76a9f27a8b88a3cf9c28ea04cc48"} {"question" "q-81710" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "5\\,\\text{ft}/\\text{s}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010777/state.json" "file_sha256" "08db95fde8ad568f4c22a52004e0b71ebaee76a9f27a8b88a3cf9c28ea04cc48"} {"question" "q-81709" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in $\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2},$ given by the function $a(t)=9{e}^{-3t}-4{e}^{-t}$, where $t$ is the time in seconds. If the particle has velocity $v=1\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}$ when $t=0\\,\\text{s}$, find the velocity (in $\\text{m/s}$)  of the particle at time $t.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010777/state.json" "file_sha256" "08db95fde8ad568f4c22a52004e0b71ebaee76a9f27a8b88a3cf9c28ea04cc48"} {"question" "q-81709" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (9{e}^{-3t}-4{e}^{-t})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =9(-\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-3t})-4(-{e}^{-t})+C \\\\  & =-3{e}^{-3t}+4{e}^{-t}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(0)=1.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =-3{e}^{0}+4{e}^{0}+C \\\\ 1 & =-3+4+C \\\\ C & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=4{e}^{-t}-3{e}^{-3t}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010777/state.json" "file_sha256" "08db95fde8ad568f4c22a52004e0b71ebaee76a9f27a8b88a3cf9c28ea04cc48"} {"question" "q-81709" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010777/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "97bf1afa6ceed68565479fe95947852ea820cfb8ac2cec4ad8694941a33898d7"} {"question" "q-81709" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010777/state.json" "file_sha256" "08db95fde8ad568f4c22a52004e0b71ebaee76a9f27a8b88a3cf9c28ea04cc48"} {"question" "q-81709" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "v(t)=4-3{e}^{-2t}"] ["math" "v(t)=4{e}^{-2t}-27{e}^{-4t}+24"] ["math" "v(t)=4{e}^{-t}-27{e}^{-3t}+24"] ["math" "v(t)=4{e}^{-t}-3{e}^{-3t}"] ["math" "v(t)=4{e}^{-t}-3{e}^{-3t}+1"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010777/state.json" "file_sha256" "08db95fde8ad568f4c22a52004e0b71ebaee76a9f27a8b88a3cf9c28ea04cc48"} {"question" "q-81709" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "v(t)=4{e}^{-t}-3{e}^{-3t}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010777/state.json" "file_sha256" "08db95fde8ad568f4c22a52004e0b71ebaee76a9f27a8b88a3cf9c28ea04cc48"} {"question" "e-562" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in $\\textrm m / \\textrm s^2,$ given by the function $a(t) = 4t^2 - 3t^3,$ where $t$ is the time in seconds. If the particle has velocity $v=2\\,\\textrm m / \\textrm s$ when $t=0 \\,\\textrm s,$ calculate the velocity (in $\\textrm m / \\textrm s$) of the particle at time $t.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010777/example-562.json" "file_sha256" "c385314ab21658200d7db36483d12f774e44c220e54fca2441604e1596a3f9a0"} {"question" "e-562" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (4{t}^{2}-3{t}^{3})\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}{t}^{3}-\\frac{3}{4}{t}^{4}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(0) = 2.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =\\frac{4}{3}(0)^{3}-\\frac{3}{4}(0)^{4}+C \\\\ C & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t) = \\dfrac{4}{3}t^3 -\\dfrac{3}{4}t^4 + 2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010777/example-562.json" "file_sha256" "c385314ab21658200d7db36483d12f774e44c220e54fca2441604e1596a3f9a0"} {"question" "e-563" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in $\\textrm m / \\textrm s^2,$ given by the function $a(t) =2e^{-t}+6t^2$, where $t$ is the time in seconds. If the particle has velocity $v=-4 \\,\\textrm m / \\textrm s$ when $t=0 \\,\\textrm s,$ calculate the speed of the particle at the moment  $t=1\\,\\textrm{s}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010777/example-563.json" "file_sha256" "2095cb79fd50f936391ec7de85009fbd55b69d5abdbaa8502212b214f4c61521"} {"question" "e-563" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (2{e}^{-t}+6{t}^{2})\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2(-{e}^{-t})+\\frac{6}{3}{t}^{3}+C \\\\  & =-2{e}^{-t}+2{t}^{3}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(0) = -4.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4 & =-2{e}^{-0}+2\\cdot {0}^{3}+C \\\\ -4 & =-2+0+C \\\\ C & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t) = -2  e^{-t}+ 2t^3 -2.$\n\nWe evaluate the velocity at time $t=1\\,\\textrm{s}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(1) & =-2{e}^{-1}+2(1)^{3}-2 \\\\  & =-2{e}^{-1}+2-2 \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{e}\\,\\text{m/s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the speed is equal to $|v(t)|,$ we conclude that the speed of the particle is $\\dfrac 2 e\\,\\textrm{m/s}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010777/example-563.json" "file_sha256" "2095cb79fd50f936391ec7de85009fbd55b69d5abdbaa8502212b214f4c61521"}] "usage" {"e-563" [] "e-562" []} "basis" 1155 "retractions" [[17592186082927 :question/problem "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in $\\textrm m / \\textrm s^2$, given by the function $a(t) = 4t^2 - 3t^3$, where $t$ is the time in seconds. If the particle has velocity $v=2\\,\\textrm m / \\textrm s$ when $t=0 \\,\\textrm s$, calculate the velocity (in $\\textrm m / \\textrm s$) of the particle at time $t$."] [17592186082927 :question/worked-solution "First, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv(t) &= ∫a(t)dt \\\\\n&= ∫(4t^{2} - 3t^{3})dt \\\\\n&= \\frac{4}{3}t^{3} - \\frac{3}{4}t^{4} + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(0) = 2$. Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2 &= \\frac{4}{3}(0)^{3} - \\frac{3}{4}(0)^{4} + C \\\\\nC &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is\n\n$$\nv(t) = \\dfrac{4}{3}t^3 -\\dfrac{3}{4}t^4 + 2\n$$"] [17592186082930 :question/problem "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in $\\textrm m / \\textrm s^2$, given by the function $a(t) =2e^{-t}+6t^2$, where $t$ is the time in seconds. If the particle has velocity $v=-4 \\,\\textrm m / \\textrm s$ when $t=0 \\,\\textrm s$, calculate the speed of the particle at the moment $t=1\\,\\textrm{s}$."] [17592186082930 :question/worked-solution "First, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv(t) &= ∫a(t)dt \\\\\n&= ∫(2e^{-t} + 6t^{2})dt \\\\\n&= 2(-e^{-t}) + \\frac{6}{3}t^{3} + C \\\\\n&=-2e^{-t} + 2t^{3} + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(0) = -4$. Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-4 &= -2e^{-0} + 2 \\cdot 0^{3} + C \\\\\n- 4 &= -2 + 0 + C \\\\\nC &= -2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is\n\n$$\nv(t) = -2 e^{-t}+ 2t^3 -2\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the velocity at time\n\n$$\nt=1\\,\\textrm{s}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv(1) &= -2e^{-1} + 2(1)^{3} - 2 \\\\\n&=-2e^{-1} + 2 - 2 \\\\\n&=-\\frac{2}{e}m/s\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the speed is equal to $\\mid v(t) \\mid$, we conclude that the speed of the particle is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 2 e\\,\\textrm{m/s}\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [] "immutable_fields" [] "no_history" []}
